#### Example in C# (Delegates):
In C#, delegates are used to explicitly define methods that can be assigned and invoked. To check for explicit delegation, examine where delegates or assignments are defined and used.

```csharp
// Example of explicit delegate assignment in C#
public delegate void Operation(int num);

public class Example
{
    public Operation operationDelegate;

    public void AssignDelegate()
    {
        // Explicit delegate assignment
        operationDelegate = PrintNumber;
    }

    public void ExecuteDelegate(int value)
    {
        // Check if delegate is assigned before invocation
        if (operationDelegate != null)
        {
            operationDelegate(value);
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Delegate not assigned.");
        }
    }

    public void PrintNumber(int number)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Number: {number}");
    }
}

// Usage
Example example = new Example();
example.AssignDelegate(); // Explicitly assigns PrintNumber to the delegate
example.ExecuteDelegate(42); // Executes the assigned delegate
```

In this example:
- Check for `operationDelegate` to see if it has been explicitly assigned.
- Use null checks to ensure proper assignment before invoking the delegate.

